# Sonification vs. Binaural Beats: A Critical Distinction

Binaural auditory beat stimulation (BABS) and sonification are fundamentally different auditory interventions that must not be conflated.

## Binaural Auditory Beat Stimulation

A psychoacoustic phenomenon: two pure tones of slightly different frequencies presented separately to each ear. The brain perceives a third beat at the difference frequency. Proposed to entrain cortical oscillations (top-down neuromodulation). The stimulus IS the intervention. Direction: stimulus changes the listener's neural state.

## Sonification

The transformation of data or movement into sound. In the thesis context: real-time conversion of sine-wave motor template or movement data into an auditory signal. Mechanism: closed-loop auditory feedback supporting motor learning via error detection and timing cues. The MOVEMENT generates the sound. Direction: movement drives sound, which feeds back to guide learning.

## Why This Distinction Matters

1. Different mechanisms: BABS = entrainment hypothesis. Sonification = error feedback and predictive motor control.
2. 2. Different experimental roles: BABS delivers a fixed stimulus before/during a task. Sonification derives sound from the task itself.
   3. 3. Different outcome logic: BABS asks 'does X Hz change cognition?' Sonification asks 'does hearing your movement improve it?'
      4. 4. Different translational claims: This scoping review does NOT claim binaural beats are sonification. The translational link is indirect: BABS research identifies which neural states and frequencies are relevant to motor control; sonification addresses how to deliver task-relevant auditory feedback.
        
